WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.989 --> 00:00:05.180
It's now been more than a quarter of a century
since Daniel Stern played Marv in Home Alone,

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:10.040
yet it remains his most recognizable role,
despite the fact that he's never stopped acting.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.960
Stern's stayed busy, so why don't we see him
as often anymore?

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:20.040
Stern was the narrator on The Wonder Years
between 1988 and 1993, and he directed a handful

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.280
of episodes along the way.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.529
Soon enough, he decided he wanted to take
a shot at directing a movie as well.

00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:31.080
So in 1993, he helmed what would be his first,
and last, feature: the family baseball comedy

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.020
Rookie of the Year.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:36.720
It's the story of a 12-year-old pitcher who
leads the Chicago Cubs to glory after discovering

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.520
that his recently broken arm has healed with
super strength.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.710
That's the sort of logic that you don't think
to question as a kid, but the grown-up critics

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.940
reviewing the film weren't as easily impressed.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:51.160
Despite the poor reviews, the film was a decent
box office success, racking up over $50 million

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:51.960
in ticket sales.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:56.540
Stern, who also acted in the movie as pitching
coach Phil Brickma, went on to direct a few

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:00.680
TV episodes here and there afterwards, but
Hollywood has yet to call him back in from

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:01.260
the bullpen.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:06.520
The box office success of the Home Alone movies
meant that Stern had little choice but to

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.480
carry on as a goofball in other films.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:13.580
He reprised his City Slickers role in the
1994 sequel The Legend of Curly's Gold, and

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:17.440
he hammed it up in forgotten comedies like
Bushwacked and Celtic Pride.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:21.940
He then decided to take radical action to
shake off his typecasting as he signed on

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:25.140
for the controversial 1998 comedy Very Bad
Things.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.780
The film is about a group of men on a bachelor
weekend trying to dispose of a prostitute

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:31.220
one of them accidentally killed.

00:01:31.549 --> 00:01:36.040
It was supposed to be edgy, but for the majority
of critics, it crossed the line into bad taste

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:36.880
far too often.

00:01:37.109 --> 00:01:40.939
Roger Ebert, for one, called it "reprehensible"
in his one-star review.

00:01:40.939 --> 00:01:46.409
Stern, however, did think it was funny, telling
the AV Club in a 2014 interview that he "enjoyed

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:50.080
it thoroughly," but the gamble didn't exactly
pay off for his career.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.980
"He's gonna take out my mini van!"

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:56.600
"Don't you dare!"

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:57.140
"Hey!

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:57.640
Hey!

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:58.140
Hey!"

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:03.300
After Very Bad Things, Stern's next credit
was a little-known crime caper called Viva

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.360
Las Nowhere, which failed to open any new
doors for him.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:11.160
From the outside it looked as though his time
was up, but while his onscreen career was

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.440
floundering, he was prospering behind the
scenes as a voice artist.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.100
Stern did everything from Burger King and
7 Up commercials to Emmy-winning shows.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:23.670
He found quite the good fit as the title character
in the TV adaption of the Dilbert comic strip

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.640
between 1999 and 2000.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:29.760
His other notable voice credits include the
likes of Hey Arnold! and Family Guy.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:35.180
Perhaps the biggest reason most of us haven't
seen Daniel Stern in a long time is that he

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:38.260
went through a six-year stretch without appearing
in any feature films.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:44.180
His last credit before 2018 came in 2012 when
he returned to the realm of festive comedy

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:45.780
with A Christmas Story 2.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:47.820
But this was no Home Alone, however.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:49.060
Far from it, in fact.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:54.680
A straight-to-DVD sequel to a much-loved yuletide
classic, A Christmas Story 2 was released

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:58.980
just days before a musical version of the
original was set to debut on Broadway.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:03.120
Warner Bros. was likely attempting to cash
in on the hype surrounding the stage production.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:07.420
Unfortunately, hardly anyone noticed, and
the few critics that bothered to review the

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.620
film were less than enthusiastic about it.

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:14.920
In 2018, Stern made a not-so-glorious return
to the screen in the Netflix original Game

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:19.000
Over, Man!, starring the Workaholics guys
as hotel housekeepers who find themselves

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.280
in a Die Hard scenario.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:24.590
The reviews were pretty much just as dreadful
as they were for A Christmas Story 2.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.440
"I'll make it up let me just get my head together
and we'll talk about it all right?"

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.060
Stern might be proud of his own career accomplishments,
but that's nothing compared to what he felt

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:37.900
after helping his son become the youngest
California State Senator in history.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:43.640
In 2016, the then 34-year-old Henry Stern
won the honor of representing the 27th District,

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.920
which stretches from Malibu to the Santa Clarita
Valley.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:51.240
A Democrat, the younger Stern is the first
millennial to make it to the California Senate,

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.200
and he plans on representing his age group
well during his term.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:58.620
One of Henry's biggest supporters on the trail
was his father, who would regularly turn up

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:01.520
to support him at campaign events and endorse
him via social media.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:08.860
When Henry won, the elder Stern could barely
contain his joy, revealing that he cried when

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:12.920
In an Instagram post, he wrote, "I have done
all I can for my own children and they are

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:14.160
children no more."

